	'pc_title' => 'The Distillation Process',
	'pc_intro' => 'Every batch at Taruki Distillery spans nine days in our three-hundred-litre copper pot still, yielding exactly four hundred and twenty bottles. We follow six unhurried stages from raw botanical preparation to hand bottling.',
	'pc_1_t' => 'Botanicals',
	'pc_1_b' => 'Our production cycle begins with the careful sorting and weighing of nine distinct botanicals. Citrus peel harvested by Samir Haddad at the Hillside Grove arrives fresh, where we peel and dry portions of the harvest on timber racks. Dried juniper berries, coriander seeds, angelica root, and local sea fennel are measured by hand. Every botanical is inspected for moisture and essential oil density before entering the copper kettle, ensuring our botanical foundation remains consistent across every seasonal distillation run.',
	'pc_2_t' => 'Steep',
	'pc_2_b' => 'Once measured, our botanicals undergo a controlled maceration inside neutral spirit. Dense roots such as angelica and orris steep alongside juniper berries and coriander seed to release their deep oils and earthy base notes. Meanwhile, lighter aromatics like delicate citrus peels, bay leaves, and pink pepper are suspended or added later in the steeping schedule to protect their fragile top notes. This gentle infusion extracts the broad aromatic profile before heat touches the kettle.',
	'pc_3_t' => 'Still',
	'pc_3_b' => 'Distillation takes place entirely in our custom three-hundred-litre copper pot still, named Ottilie. Tomasz Wren manages the slow, gentle heating cycle, keeping temperatures moderate so the spirit vapour rises steadily through the copper neck. The interaction between spirit vapour and solid copper strips harsh sulphur compounds and softens the texture. We deliberately run Ottilie slower than conventional stills, allowing subtle floral esters and harbour citrus brightness to carry over into the condenser cleanly.',
	'pc_4_t' => 'Cut',
	'pc_4_b' => 'The cut is the defining sensory moment of our distillation. Head distiller Mira Okafor stands at Ottilie\'s collection spout with a small tasting glass, separating the run by aroma, temperature, and taste. The harsh heads containing pungent volatile esters flow away, and only the balanced, aromatic heart of the run is collected. As the run shifts toward heavier tails, Mira makes the final cut, preserving crisp citrus and warm spice while eliminating muddy bitter notes.',
	'pc_5_t' => 'Rest',
	'pc_5_b' => 'After the distillation cut is completed, the new-make spirit is not rushed into glass. The spirit rests undisturbed in stainless steel conditioning tanks for several days. This resting period allows the newly concentrated ethanol molecules and delicate aromatic botanical oils to marry, soften, and stabilise. We monitor clarity and aroma daily as the vibrant citrus oils integrate seamlessly with the deep earthy roots, producing our signature rounded and cohesive mouthfeel.',
	'pc_6_t' => 'Bottle',
